#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161278 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161278 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c59a886f-a8e9-5c1a-83c5-0a1690532273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161279 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161279 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161279 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c75b2b50-3ec3-5808-b5ec-a1a4851138b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161280 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161280 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161280 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1b5d11f-d085-5957-ab58-25b88f1e8f53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161281 #edb/ref 1080 "\\sqrt{13}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161281 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161281 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f8dbebe-20bc-5b8e-a384-b6ffc2c89a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161288 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "50d8446e-fd5b-543b-bc96-f048b71511a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161808 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161808 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e5/e56fbd2ced76bc02d7c9b29a81f3afab000053d11ec878040f26cef6cc5f65ce.svg)\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$\n![](images/ba/ba1b9ecff7b4825d7eb4fa25ad706c14d04a82b8daa45e524d556c90d71700ca.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161808 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "232a0173-d743-5696-a6d9-c8279514bcba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161808 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that since $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186161809 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161809 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f0/f0cb7d70ea0921d1e2e8f2f0a9d8bc06427f29e28b8ed6ea79b5db4ba132f1e0.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161809 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad5570e6-4810-5b07-a676-711f0fd069a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161809 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_i$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186161810 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161810 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cb/cb8af25c129aac1ef9268d108c073e4b895f5e154c26ddb5692fd527d4eaddc3.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d861ebc5-8e1e-5cf0-9b82-5a64ff80cf96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161810 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=25.$ Computing $A^T \\! A - 25 I,$ we get\n\n![](images/8c/8c5b88c719ee0e5495e9b0df2c9f4add97a668e74249680fbf378c7c775d92f2.svg)\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T\\!A-25I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/b3/b36812322e6f9128de625a8a2d4e7faf62ba98fb007224c819e2a05738cd7f98.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we get \n![](images/16/1608541af7edcbbb06504c97b907dc2c40a97d9a7c9575d5145c67785f9eb56c.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|v_{11}|+|v_{21}| = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| = \\sqrt{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161811 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161811 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/eb/eb6e660e7dd1c933fd2d5c6b6a05fbdf1fe7f5f8ed24faf15f88ea16d7d04889.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161811 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "828e62a9-0076-5ff8-8220-4f779d48eb65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161811 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{bmatrix}-81\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-72 & -24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/74/748df21fe8314e02f5a5253edaa39427c38600cd60099ccdfed075a28499d23e.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/08/08573d10464e356dc2eeb7a3a0a4c5f9255d0f96b14da9d63b718b766c1cc8d8.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/252867b0bccd95be918f05320ee65c7153f499b15ccc77624be18128d0f0de09.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162016 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186543517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 