 17592186045417] [17592186371296 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371296 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371296 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b42e903-5007-58dd-9a44-08bf6ca76aac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454869 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186371318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454870 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186371318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454871 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186371318 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371318 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371318 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Σ$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix\n- **II.** $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix with only positive elements on the main diagonal\n- **III.** $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186371318 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3302b4c-3bdd-5b04-9e3e-aac900425c6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371318 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $5\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $Σ$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be diagonal with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal. The main diagonal may contain zeroes.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371339 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371339 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371339 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371339 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371339 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c77fff6-4214-509e-aadd-6db989607130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371339 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371340 #edb/re