86371381 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371381 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371381 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371381 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371381 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f93258a8-eb4b-5688-9b20-35af35814e98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371381 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 17\\end{bmatrix}-25\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-9I & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 17\\end{bmatrix}-9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371382 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186